**...and looks for a new platform to build…** Japan’s evident need to re-build and the concomitant impact on metals demand across the board will be factors for a later discussion.  (As a comparison, China’s government took six months to identify the need to spend US$150Bn to re-build parts of Sichuan province after the 8.0 Wenchuan earthquake of May 12 2008, and that expenditure was to be carried out over the following three years.)  But for now, copper prices remain strong even if they have ebbed away from their earlier US$10,000/t average target.  Indeed, they broke through US$9700/t ($4.40/lb) again as late as the 25<sup>th</sup> , despite several conditions that suggest they should, at best, consolidate. 

